<Base title="118 vs 135 degree drill point" description="118° points are the general-purpose jobber grind. 135° split points wander less in steel and are common on cobalt bits. Point angle is not drill diameter." path="/point-118-vs-135/">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / 118° vs 135°</p>
    <h1>118° vs 135° drill points</h1>
    <Answer>
      Point angle does not change the diameter. 118° is the common jobber grind for wood, plastic, and mild steel. 135° split-point bits start easier in steel and are what most cobalt sets use.
    </Answer>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>118°</th><th>135° split</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Starts</td><td>Wants a center punch or a smaller pilot</td><td>Self-centering on most steel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Materials</td><td>Wood, plastic, aluminum, mild steel</td><td>Steel, stainless, harder alloys</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Look</td><td>Sharper nose</td><td>Flatter nose, often a split web</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>If the hole size is wrong, change the bit diameter — not the point angle.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
